WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.357 --> 00:00:06.850
RODERICK YAO: Hello everyone.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.690
Welcome to my talk about
architecting and building

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.170
a data lake on GCP
with open source tools.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:16.079
My name is Roderick Yao, I'm a
Cloud data engineer at Google

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.460
professional services.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.620
We help our most
strategic customers

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.490
migrating to GCP and take
most advantage of GCP.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.980
I work with many of the
customer across many industries,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.200
from marketing,
retail, technology,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.850
to financial services.

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.960
So Hadoop data lakes today
are the most common ones

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.440
that is on-prem.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.340
So most of our [? in-price ?]
customer are moving Hadoop

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.700
to GCP, and the trend
is accelerating.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.550
So today I'll walk you through
how to architect and build

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.320
data lakes on GCP and what
principles, tools, and patterns

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.690
that you should choose
to build data lakes.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.090
On top of that, it
is important to know

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:57.750
how to secure and
govern a data lake.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.595
So why should I run
data lakes on GCP?

00:01:00.595 --> 00:01:01.970
So there are many
challenges that

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.220
gain value out of the
on-premise data lakes today.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.160
The total cost of ownership
is very high, higher

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.610
than expected.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.280
There are many hidden costs.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:12.560
It's hard to project.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.750
It's hard to scale, although
Hadoop is also built

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.570
on the principle of scale.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.570
Governance and the security
usually is an afterthought,

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.550
and management burdens
are high on the teams.

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.300
The admin teams are struggling
with many tasks such as tuning

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.400
or scaling or
resource allocation,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.160
guiding the application
teams, et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.730
The list goes on.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.060
So by moving data
lakes to Cloud,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.940
you can extend
existing investments.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.980
In on-premise environment,
the cluster size

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.100
is almost fixed tuned
for maximum capacity.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.980
In Cloud, you have the chance
to optimize for the cost

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:50.670
by spinning up and down clusters
and configure them separately.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.390
So also in the Cloud,
you can scale easily.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.450
No more take months to
get the capacity you need.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.880
By moving some of the work
to managed components,

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.530
it can help reduce workload
for your big data team

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:08.060
and let users focus on add
more values more directly.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:11.180
By utilizing more integrated
tools and existing patterns,

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.520
enable democratization
and elect more teams

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.490
to get access to data
they need, and finally,

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:20.120
by running more workloads
on natively-build tools

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:23.630
and platform, your team
can develop things quicker

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.150
and requires less maintenance
and more connectives

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.500
through the GCP platform.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.470
So let's talk
about how to design

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:35.573
and migrating data lakes to GCP.

00:02:35.573 --> 00:02:37.740
Because we're talking about
mostly Hadoop data lakes

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.580
for an on-prem customer,
that's what we most guide

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.635
our customers for,
there are many tools

00:02:44.635 --> 00:02:46.380
to choose from to
build a data lake.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.225
For example, these are just the
data [? analytics ?] product

00:02:49.225 --> 00:02:51.260
in GCP.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.750
I will walk you through
how to choose and build

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.430
a data lake using
these tools and also

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.340
the tools of your
choice of course.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.810
Our guiding principles here
are open, integrate, and safe.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.980
So open tool, many open
source big data tools

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.490
add file formats out of the box,
avoiding lock-ins and creating

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.720
another silo.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.220
We want to make
sure the data lake's

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.500
more connected to the
other tools and capacities

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.720
such as Apache Spark, Hive
TensorFlow, and various GCP ML

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.630
AI APIs, et cetera, et cetera.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.770
The third is safe.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.600
Safe to migrate
without having to make

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.050
risky decisions while getting
benefit from the Cloud.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.630
A couple of key considerations
when you migrating

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.160
stuff when you're planning.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.010
So be clear about your near
term and long term goals

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:37.270
and priorities.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:41.040
For example, if your data center
lease is ending in two months,

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.140
your goal should probably
get your data center

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.170
or get your clusters out of
there as soon as possible

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:50.230
rather than making
significant architecture

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.150
changes along the way.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.390
The best way to utilize GCP
is to choose the right tool

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.240
for the workload instead of
finding just the replacement

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.090
tool for every tool
you have today.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.600
It is best to utilize GCS's
separate storage and compute.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:07.045
This will enable you to use
more ephemeral [? cluster ?]

00:04:07.045 --> 00:04:11.100
or another type of Cloud
storage pattern to lower cost,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.110
improve performance,
and scale better.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:17.800
Last is you need to rethink
about your operations.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.350
You will use a bunch
of different types

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.240
of cluster and services.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.400
The old on-premise
management tools

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.900
such as Apache and
[INAUDIBLE] just

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.620
wouldn't help in that case.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.880
You want to make your
infrastructure and application

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.600
developments as code, so
your application teams

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.750
can deploy them because
they know it better.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:41.260
Most of our customer choose
to use Apache Airflow or Cloud

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.470
Composer to run their platforms.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.620
Airflow packages most
of the operations

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.450
that you need to interact with
the Cloud providers products

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:53.610
into a different
Airflow operators.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:55.870
Too many cluster
creation and deletion

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:58.330
could result in higher overhead.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.270
For example, jobs
could take a little bit

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:03.820
longer to get started
and then shut it down.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.430
So pooling clusters together
and add a middle layer

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:11.030
to choose a less utilized
cluster is a good idea.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:12.500
There are some tools out there.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:17.350
For example, Spotify creates an
open source tool called Spydra.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.670
It's available on GitHub.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.520
So let's take a look at the
usual workload patterns.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.020
We generally have these
four types of workload.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:30.250
It is best to know what type of
workloads that you're running.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:32.580
So these profiles
and patterns will

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.720
help you choose which GCP tool
to use and what type of cluster

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.675
to run and how you should
implement security and data

00:05:40.675 --> 00:05:41.175
governance.

00:05:41.175 --> 00:05:44.710
So this part is very important.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:46.560
So I like to discuss
technology choices

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:49.180
into these five different
layers of a data lake.

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.470
So let's start with the storage.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:55.920
The main storage for a data lake
is the Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.070
It has many features
listed over here.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:03.098
The most important ones are
it has immediate consistency.

00:06:03.098 --> 00:06:05.265
One file as an object has
[INAUDIBLE] in through GCS

00:06:05.265 --> 00:06:08.370
is mailed immediately,
available for listing.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.200
So this part is
very, very important

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:13.420
for many Hadoop and
Spark workloads.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:18.240
It helps facilitate many
transient or ephemeral clusters

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:20.760
by separate the
storage and compute.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:24.990
There's no need for three times
replication such as in HDFS,

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:29.660
and you only need to
pay for what you use.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.530
A little bit more
on GCS connector.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.360
It's designed to support
Hadoop ecosystem workload

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.960
and working with GCS.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:38.830
It's transparent to
use as a application,

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.640
so you can use whatever
format that you have.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.540
It has improved support
for Parquet file

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.270
format for analytic workloads.

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:52.540
We can simulate atomic
directory operations

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:54.190
using cooperative locking.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.790
So there's a little bit
more technical details

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:01.870
in the block here below.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:05.670
So the best practice
for renting a storage.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.600
So small files has
some problem for HDFS.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.460
It creates a pressure
for name nodes.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.250
Before GCS, too many
small files were

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.400
resolved like too many round
trips through the network,

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.010
so that adds up latency.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.470
Always use the new
version of connectors

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.410
to take advantage of
the new improvements.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.410
We're making them constantly.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.420
Align your Parquet file
block size to 2 megabytes

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:35.220
because that's what the
GCS block size defaults to.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.780
Always partition your data
to gain full-term benefits.

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:43.790
And regarding the
migration, the CRC checksum

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:48.030
are calculated differently
for HDFS and GCS

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:49.720
for the same object.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.830
So we applied some
changes to that

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.020
to help you establish checksums.

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:59.550
In order to use those, so make
sure to use a Hadoop version

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:02.190
that's newer than 2.9.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.770
If you don't have the
ability to upgrade,

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:06.960
cluster to a newer version.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.590
You can choose a
workaround which

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:12.540
uses MD5 checksum to move to
a Dataproc cluster with HDFS

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:13.630
first.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:17.130
Then move those files to GCS.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:19.830
Regarding the
migration tools, DistCp

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:24.090
is the easiest way to
move data into GCS.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.270
If you have HBase or
Cassandra in your cluster,

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:31.520
you can consider move
them to Cloud BigTable.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.222
BigTable is a managed service.

00:08:34.222 --> 00:08:36.450
It provides you
managed replication.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.179
It's really easy to scale up.

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:43.200
It's fully compatible
with HBase APIs.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:49.620
It has a nice data for templates
to help you to uploading.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.210
One of the exceptions.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:54.510
If you're using Apache
Phoenix, the data model,

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:59.473
it's actually similar to a Cloud
Spanner than Cloud BigTable.

00:08:59.473 --> 00:09:01.140
So you can consider
move your data there

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.180
if you want to use a
managed tool for it.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:09.840
If you only use Hadoop for
data warehouse use cases

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.200
and dealing with
only structured data

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.180
where it's a little bit
more semi-structured data,

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.130
you can consider
implement your data lakes

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.300
using BigQuery plus
BigQuery storage.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:23.130
BigQuery storage API really
treats data warehouse

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:27.840
like a storage, and allow you
to use BigQuery like a data

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.720
warehouse plus a data lake.

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:33.750
So it integrates well with
many, many tools in GCP

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.540
and also open source ecosystem,
such as Apache Spark,

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:42.030
Hive TensorFlow and many
of the Cloud AI products.

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:45.900
So next part, we talk
about the Metastore.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.780
Hive Metastore is a service
that stores metadata

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.730
about time tables like
schema and location

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:55.100
and partitions in the
relational database.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.910
It's actually the
de-facto standard

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.580
for many of the SQL
engines on Hadoop

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:03.540
and also the newer trend
table formats such as Delta

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:07.860
Lake, Apache Iceberg,
and Apache Hudi.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.120
Having the centralized Hive
Metastore with data on GCS

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.360
enable separate storage
and computes further.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.630
You can use multiple cluster
operate on different Hive

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.900
tables simultaneously.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:25.120
We just announced the alpha
of Dataproc Metastore.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:27.680
It's actually a
managed Hive Metastore.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:30.730
You can refer to
the talk about that.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:34.050
So you can also map
your BigQuery tables

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:35.540
into Hive Metastore.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.340
So you can use
something like this

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.430
and use a Spark BigQuery
connector to read data

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:43.900
from BigQuery.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.440
Next, it's about
ingestion later.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:51.010
Many of you are using maybe
Apache Flume, Apache Scoop

00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:55.520
or third-party tools for
free ingesting data into GCP.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:00.130
Google actually offers a
fully managed open source

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:01.410
tool called Data Fusion.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.860
It's based on the
[INAUDIBLE] app.

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:08.570
It's a fully managed code-free
data ingestion service

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.050
for building and
managing data pipelines.

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.630
It has pretty deep
GCP integrations

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.540
with many type of
sources and syncs.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.730
If you prefer, you
can still continue

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:24.170
to use Apache Flume and
Scoop together with Dataproc,

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:26.740
and it will also supports
many of the third-party tools

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:30.590
like Talon or Infomatica.

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:33.260
Next part about
processing layer.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:37.850
This is mostly based
on your team's skills.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:41.600
You can choose from one of
these two processing frameworks.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.520
So Dataproc is the
managed Hadoop framework,

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:51.860
so it has many of the open
source data processing

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:55.170
firmware such as Spark,
Flink, Hive, Pig, and et

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:56.750
cetera, et cetera.

00:11:56.750 --> 00:12:01.280
Dataflow is a service
framework using Apache Beam,

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.770
so it's a different programming
paradigm over there.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:10.050
So the best practice of
choosing a processing framework.

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:13.680
When migrating, make
sure you at least

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.220
recompile your
application because you

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:20.850
want to match the Java version,
Spark, and Scala version

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:24.190
to the Dataproc image
version that you have.

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:30.690
For example, Dataproc 1.5 uses
Scala 2.12 version of Spark.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:34.650
So it's maybe different
than the one you're using.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:39.270
So at least recompile your
application to validate that.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:42.600
When you deploy an
application on Dataproc,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:46.230
autoscaling is good when you
don't know the exact size

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:48.520
of the cluster that you need.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:52.740
But if you do know the job that
will need more than x number

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.690
of nodes, then start with that.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:56.970
Don't just start with
the autoscaling from one

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:57.660
node cluster.

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:00.770
It will take very long
time to get there.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:02.760
It has flexibility modes.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.780
Allow you to store
shuffled data on HDFS

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:09.720
as a primer of workloads to
avoid job failures and restarts

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:11.640
when some of the nodes fail.

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.250
You might want to
consider use that.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:19.730
E2 VMs and preemptible VMs are
great for drive down the cost.

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:24.910
But for preempted VMs, you're
having a risk of job failure

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:29.100
or lose progress when the
preempted Vms goes away.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:35.830
Dataproc jobs API provides
authorization, authentication,

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:37.660
and auditing for jobs.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:41.410
So you can consider using that
for submitting jobs to cluster.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:44.380
Next, we can talk about
the data warehouse choice.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:47.380
Native GCP data
warehouse is BigQuery.

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:49.630
But you can always
run open source query

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:54.040
engines such as Hive, Presto,
and others on Dataproc.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.800
Customer found the best value
after migrating data warehouse

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:01.010
to BigQuery, where you implement
self-service data warehouse.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.770
BigQuery requires no operation.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:07.660
It is fully managed with
flexible resource allocation

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:08.500
and pricing model.

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:10.240
It supports multiple
file formats

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:13.877
that you can find and be able
to query them and ingest them

00:14:13.877 --> 00:14:14.460
into BigQuery.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:21.300
So when migrating a database
to BigQuery, in many case,

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.420
we scope a BigQuery
migration proof of concept,

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:26.250
even if the first
stage of migration

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:30.000
is a [INAUDIBLE] shift, just
to demonstrate the values.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.190
The difference between the
existing data warehouse

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:35.370
and BigQuery may sounds
big at the beginning,

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:39.000
but for processing and
reporting of structure data,

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.880
most of our customer found
great value after PoC

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.040
and roll out more users
within two or three

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:47.560
months, sometimes just in a
matter of four to five weeks.

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:51.330
So what makes the migration
to BigQuery simple?

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:55.860
Migration is not just migrating
the storage and the engine

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:59.580
alone, it also is not
done until upstream

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:01.320
and downstream
pipeline components

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.700
are identified
and also migrated.

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:06.600
So a move to BigQuery
is easy for most people

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:11.810
because the connector exists for
very simple usage such as Spark

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:15.510
and BigQuery connectors, Hive
BigQuery storage handlers.

00:15:15.510 --> 00:15:18.140
For downstream
consumptions, BigQuery

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:22.650
supports most of the BI
tools by JDBC and ODBC.

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:27.390
For many, BigQuery just simpler
and better user experience.

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:31.260
We'll see how the security and
data governance in the next few

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:32.070
slides.

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:34.020
Typically, you need
Kerberos and Ranger

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:38.460
to confidently secure Hadoop
data lake or data warehouse.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:41.610
For BigQuery, it's all built in
and integrated with IM already.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:48.760
Couple of the best
practices when migrating.

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:52.765
BigQuery supports data and
integer range partitioning.

00:15:52.765 --> 00:15:55.690
It supports clustering
multiple columns.

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:57.550
However, this is
different than Hive.

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:59.280
So if your Hive table
is not partitioned

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:04.000
by data or through a range, you
want to repartition and load

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:05.980
the data to BigQuery.

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.860
Normally, you can [INAUDIBLE]
performance anyway just to

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:10.800
be sure.

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:13.630
BigQuery uses
standard [? NC ?] SQL.

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:16.000
There will be some
minor differences

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:17.130
for some of the functions.

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:21.490
But largely, it is compatible
with most of the query.

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:24.670
But like above, you can also--

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:28.900
if your Hive uses complex
partitioning strategy,

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:32.260
you may need to change
those queries as well.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:37.440
The last, if you used to
run high ETL [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:43.720
of our scale and you also
liked BigQuery to do ETL,

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:45.810
it's better to do that
in the dedicated project

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:49.180
or dedicated slots,
allocated pool,

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:52.950
than using the same project
or the same [? slots ?] that

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:56.850
serves ad hoc queries just
to ensure a better user

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:58.150
experience.

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.660
So we have some
customers who want

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:05.400
to keep their data on-premise
and in other clouds

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:08.460
while also using GCP.

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:11.700
And they also want to use
one query engine and one

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:15.270
sets of a script to avoid
lock-ins or just create

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:17.160
another silos.

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:20.579
In this case, you can
actually use Presto for this.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:23.640
It is easy to deploy
Presto on Dataproc

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:27.220
with the BigQuery connectors.

00:17:27.220 --> 00:17:29.370
Naturally the connectors
use the BigQuery storage

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:33.310
API to provide
faster query results.

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:36.350
Presto has many other connects
through to talk to your,

00:17:36.350 --> 00:17:39.920
for example, transactional
data warehouse,

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:42.650
your other enterprise
data warehouse,

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:46.290
even legacy Hadoop clusters.

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:49.840
So next, I want to touch on
security and data governance.

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:53.550
There are many types
of identities in GCP.

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:55.140
You're familiar with
Google Identity.

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:56.730
That's when you sign in.

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.800
There are service accounts that
represents the application.

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:03.200
Let's see how that
impacts security here.

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:06.430
In a simple setup, in
this case, each user

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:08.890
has a distinct service account.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:14.620
That person has a cluster use
his and her service account.

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:18.725
These cluster have data inside
GCS they need access to.

00:18:18.725 --> 00:18:22.630
So from GCS access log
perspective, only service

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:25.020
accounts access the GCS.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:26.770
But you can look it
up who actually did it

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:30.280
because the users and
service account has mapping.

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:33.110
Access control is only
on the bucket level,

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:38.200
so you could use a conditional
IM to fine tune that.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.590
If you use jobs API,
you could find out

00:18:41.590 --> 00:18:44.160
which user actually submitted
jobs through the login,

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:46.640
but more on that later.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:50.440
In this setup with Kerberos,
we're using a shared cluster.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:55.270
We want to make sure
user A is who they are.

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.120
So users [? are ?]
indicated via Kerberos,

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:00.820
so we can stop people from
using the service that they

00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:03.010
don't have access to.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:04.660
In the background,
all the API calls

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:07.710
are made by a service
account of that cluster.

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:11.260
So Dataproc with Kerberos will
setup KDC on the master node,

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:14.980
and KDC uses one-way
trust, or cross-room trust

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:17.800
to active directory.

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:22.870
If user SSH to the cluster,
they will use their Linux user

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:24.970
to interact with
Hadoop services.

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:28.660
Before they do that, they need a
kinit to get a Kerberos ticket.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:32.490
So when they access outside
of a Dataproc cluster,

00:19:32.490 --> 00:19:34.620
it's actually using
the service account.

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:38.590
BI tools in this case, if
you're not using BigQuery,

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:42.500
they will need to gain access
to the Hive or Presto port.

00:19:42.500 --> 00:19:44.230
To be able to--

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:46.890
all the data acts
as human identity.

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:50.210
Previously, we talked about
from the access log perspective,

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:54.270
it is the service account
who access the GCS.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:56.360
We develop a source
token broker,

00:19:56.360 --> 00:20:00.220
and we presented last
year in the Cloud Next.

00:20:00.220 --> 00:20:03.290
It will translate Kerberos
principal to Google Identity

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:06.130
and call GCP APIs using that.

00:20:06.130 --> 00:20:11.350
So now you can control GCS data
for individual user access.

00:20:11.350 --> 00:20:13.270
And the access control
is still on the bucket

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:17.890
level with the
conditional IM possible.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:20.380
If you want a table-level
access control,

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.750
then you need to
use Apache Ranger.

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:25.980
So for authorization
management on data lake,

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:29.470
let's take a look
at what capabilities

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:31.310
are available in GCP.

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:35.470
On file system level, on-premise
HDFS uses POSIX permissions.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:38.710
You can continue to use
that for HDFS on GCP,

00:20:38.710 --> 00:20:43.900
but the HDFS comes
with the Dataproc only

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:45.680
for temporary storage.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:49.400
So it's more important to
look at GCS access control.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:53.080
For now, GCS only has
bucket-level access control.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:55.450
Like I said earlier,
conditional IMs

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:58.070
are available with
prefix settings,

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:00.790
but the purpose is now really
just to do fine grain access

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:03.250
control to each object level.

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:06.460
So there's a limited number
of conditions you can apply.

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:09.760
On the SQL engine side,
on-premise, we usually

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:13.600
use Apache Ranger for
restricting a certain user,

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:15.220
tables, or columns.

00:21:15.220 --> 00:21:17.890
On GCP, you can
continue to use Ranger

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:19.900
if you want to continue
to use open source SQL

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:22.660
engines such as Hive
and Presto, et cetera.

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:25.330
If you migrate your enterprise
data warehouse to BigQuery,

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:27.910
BigQuery natively
provide access control

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:32.620
on data set level, table level,
column level, and roll level.

00:21:32.620 --> 00:21:34.930
These couple of
the later features

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:37.240
are available in beta right now.

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:40.960
Another level of access control
is service access control.

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:47.980
For example, which
Hadoop service or UI

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:49.330
the user can access to.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:54.240
This is typically done by
either Hue or use Apache Knox.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:22:00.010
On Dataproc, you can use
Apache Knox gateway to do that.

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:04.020
So first, let's take a look
at how Dataproc jobs API

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:08.200
can solve these authorization
problem to some extent.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.910
While user A has
submit a job, logs

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:13.390
will show who actually
submit the job,

00:22:13.390 --> 00:22:16.210
and GCS will still
show it's a service

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:17.810
account that access the data.

00:22:17.810 --> 00:22:23.080
However, external BI application
doesn't really speak jobs API,

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:26.940
so they can only connect
to the unsecured connection

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:29.060
to the cluster.

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:33.220
So in a more complicated
setup with Ranger lets

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:37.020
you see how Ranger solved
the granular access control.

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:42.400
So Ranger can be started as
a Dataproc optional component

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.800
to use in the large
production system.

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:47.830
You might want to consider
centralized Ranger service

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:51.910
so it can be deployed
on GCE with a Cloud SQL

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:54.130
as the backend database.

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:58.510
Ranger provides database table
column-level access control

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:00.820
for HiveServer2.

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:03.430
Any request that goes
through HiveServer2

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:06.580
will be checked and
authorized by Ranger.

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:10.750
This will apply to BI tools
that connect through Hive.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:13.930
Hive also has
plugin with Presto.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:16.030
So the same thing if you--

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:21.380
BI to connect to this Presto,
Ranger can also solve that.

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:24.530
Ranger does not sync
permission to Cloud storage

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:27.020
like it did for HDFS.

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:29.630
To avoid this
issue, you can have

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:33.170
a cluster that only provides
SQL service with Ranger

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:38.010
to secure access so people
don't circumvent that.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:41.295
As mentioned before, Apache
Knox can help control service

00:23:41.295 --> 00:23:46.990
access such as allow and denying
access to YARN UI or Hue UI

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:48.280
would be something else.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:52.230
It adds access control for
a cluster used by BI tools

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:55.560
instead of letting BI tool to
connect to the ports of Hive

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:57.440
or Presto or something else.

00:23:57.440 --> 00:24:00.080
It passes user identity
to the cluster,

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:02.730
so Ranger can also apply
user-base authentication

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:07.130
and authorization
even without Kerberos.

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:10.310
Next, we talk about
metadata management tasks.

00:24:10.310 --> 00:24:12.800
To make things work together
with open source big data

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:15.440
tools, there are
some solutions here.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:18.350
GCP offers data catalog,
which catalogs data

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:20.830
from BigQuery Pub/Sub natively.

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:23.870
It also has a set of connector
to import catalog data

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:26.720
from Hive Metastore
and other systems.

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:30.260
Data Fusion provides a way of
standardized data processing

00:24:30.260 --> 00:24:33.380
with native tools,
provided data lineage.

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:35.840
For open source
component, Apache Atlas

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:39.490
can provide those sort
of lineage from that.

00:24:39.490 --> 00:24:42.380
Apache Atlas and their
catalog both provides a way

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:47.900
to tap and search your data,
automatically categorize

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:50.720
data, and match them more
crucial to processing

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:52.140
sensitive data.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:55.070
So Google's data loss
prevention API and Apache Atlas

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:57.080
both provide a way
to categorize data

00:24:57.080 --> 00:25:00.870
and match them when necessary.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:01.900
So more on Apache Atlas.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:06.270
It provides data lineage,
classification access

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:09.310
control working together
with Apache Ranger,

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:11.580
and also discovery capabilities.

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:14.480
It work with most open
source components.

00:25:14.480 --> 00:25:18.700
It also has integration with the
third-party tool like Collibra.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:21.250
So here's a way how you can
quickly create a new cluster

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:24.250
to test out the Apache
Atlas without affecting

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:25.640
other workloads.

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:27.730
So some of the
best practices when

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:30.580
we come to security
and data governance.

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:34.800
Application has
predefined behavior,

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:41.050
so these are usually informal
batch or streaming workloads.

00:25:41.050 --> 00:25:43.630
They could be long running
or they could be scheduled.

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:46.090
These are not ad
hoc jobs, so there's

00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.800
no really human
interaction in them.

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:50.530
So normally, we can
trust them running

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:54.340
and grant the minimum permission
to the service account

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:58.530
they're running, so you know
how to trace back to them.

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:03.060
For data warehouse, it
is mostly ad hoc queries

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:04.920
for these use cases.

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:08.550
Most user would just
send simple queries

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:11.700
through Hive and
[INAUDIBLE] others.

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:15.330
We recommended using
BigQuery to simplify this.

00:26:15.330 --> 00:26:19.110
But if you do need to
run open source data

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:22.020
warehouse such as
Hive or Presto,

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:25.780
you can just use
Ranger to secure that.

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:30.330
For a third type, which we
call exploratory workloads,

00:26:30.330 --> 00:26:31.550
they're specialized.

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:35.030
Maybe a data scientists
use PySpark to profile data

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:36.710
and build a model.

00:26:36.710 --> 00:26:40.730
You can set up Dataproc cluster
with distributed notebooks

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:44.030
for the personal clusters
or team clusters,

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:48.440
provided you can control
access of fast service account.

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:50.960
If that doesn't
fit, say you want

00:26:50.960 --> 00:26:54.490
to share the cluster
with a few more people,

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.930
then you can use Kerberos
together with open source

00:26:56.930 --> 00:27:00.380
token book [? here. ?]

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:04.220
So let's talk a little
about hybrid and multi-Cloud

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:09.490
scenarios because I hear
this more often these days.

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:12.670
If you really think about
it, hybrid scenarios

00:27:12.670 --> 00:27:13.910
are pretty common.

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:19.270
So every data migration
has a hybrid stage

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:23.980
where some of our data on jobs
are migrated, some are not.

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:26.470
To accommodate this
scenario, first you

00:27:26.470 --> 00:27:29.200
need to have very
good interconnect

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:33.130
because you need to move data
or jobs through both environment

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:35.060
all the time.

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:38.590
Full DNS resolution,
it's important

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:41.410
if bi-directional
communication is needed,

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:45.720
and it's crucial for
Kerberos to work too.

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:49.520
And then, use a very
powerful orchestration tool,

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:53.030
like Apache Airflow or
Composer, to launch tasks

00:27:53.030 --> 00:27:56.060
in all these different
environments.

00:27:56.060 --> 00:27:57.950
Our customer typically
elect to choose

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:00.760
one tool to control all
the environment and people

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.960
to [INAUDIBLE] to
all the environments.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:06.080
So make sure you have a
discovery service that

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:10.100
routes the workload to
the correct cluster based

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:12.690
on the request.

00:28:12.690 --> 00:28:15.960
If you don't feel like
making copies of data

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:19.920
to different environments,
some other zero copy solution

00:28:19.920 --> 00:28:23.390
like Alluxio could be
a good fit for that.

00:28:23.390 --> 00:28:27.150
OK, so that concludes my talk.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:30.960
To recap, we went through
high-level migration principles

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:34.320
of open, integrated, and safe.

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:38.850
Rules of migration, such as
establish clear priorities,

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:41.850
timeline, separating
storage and compute,

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:44.040
and rethink about operations.

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:47.430
Then we dive into the
tech stack choice.

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:50.880
We talk about storage choice
of GCS, BigQuery storage,

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:52.950
and Cloud BigTable.

00:28:52.950 --> 00:28:56.160
Data Fusion for ingestions,
Dataproc and Dataflow

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:57.750
for processing layer.

00:28:57.750 --> 00:29:00.230
For data warehouse
layer, we discuss

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:03.420
a choice of Hive,
Presto, and BigQuery

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:06.570
and what impact to consider
when moving them to BigQuery

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:10.350
and how to solve federated
environment query challenge

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:13.200
with Dataproc and Presto.

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:17.290
And then we talked about how to
secure and govern data lakes.

00:29:17.290 --> 00:29:19.260
We talked about Kerberos
authentications,

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:23.280
what's the impact on
data access and auditing,

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:28.110
how to authorize data
using Kerberos Ranger, jobs

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:30.690
API, and Apache Knox.

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:32.570
BigQuery actually
does very good job

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:36.710
with solving those access
control challenges.

00:29:36.710 --> 00:29:38.640
So definitely it's
a preferred way

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:41.830
of running data
warehouse on GCP.

00:29:41.830 --> 00:29:46.080
Last, we discuss Apache
Atlas and data catalog

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:49.300
for data governance tasks.

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:51.120
So that's pretty much it.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:53.450
Thanks for joining my talk.

